-This family defines the neural network structures used to represent the continuous radiance field $F_\Theta: (\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}) \rightarrow (\sigma, \mathbf{c})$ in differentiable volume rendering. It solves the problem of mapping 3D spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volumetric density and view-dependent color, enabling optimization through the volumetric rendering integral. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by focusing on the internal topology, input encoding, and parameterization of the field itself, rather than the sampling strategy [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md), loss functions [Regularization and Loss Functions](8586-regularization-and-loss-functions.md), or scene partitioning [Scene Representation and Partitioning](9444-scene-representation-and-partitioning.md).
+Scene Representation and Architecture defines the neural network structures used to approximate the continuous radiance field $F_\Theta: (\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}) \rightarrow (\sigma, \mathbf{c})$ in differentiable volume rendering. It solves the problem of representing 3D geometry and appearance as a differentiable function that can be optimized via gradient descent. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by focusing on the network topology and input encoding rather than the sampling strategy, and from [Scene Representation and Partitioning](9444-scene-representation-and-partitioning.md) by typically assuming a single global representation rather than decomposing the scene.
 
