 ## Background
-The canonical MLP-based radiance field representation was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S1, Parent]. This work established the use of positional encoding and coupled density/color MLPs as the standard architecture. Subsequent developments, such as MobileNeRF (June 2022) and Neural Body (Dec 2020), extended this architecture to mesh-based representations and deformable human avatars, respectively [S1].
+The canonical Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1]. It established the standard of using coupled MLPs with positional encoding to represent continuous radiance fields. Subsequent developments, such as MobileNeRF (June 2022) and EfficientNeRF (July 2022), extended this representation to mesh-based and tree-based structures for efficiency [S1].
 
